// The 20 blind-test samples.
window.ESSAYS = [["Last week I passed a car dealership on my way home from the grocery store. The cars were beautiful. They looked clean and shiny and smelled of fresh leather, or so I imagined, since I can no longer walk into a dealership and pretend to care. Last year I sold my 2016 Honda Civic for $11,500 and haven't regretted it once. That might sound crazy, because I don't live in New York - I live in Fort Worth, Texas - but it's the truth. I don't plan on buying another car ever again.", "When I told my dad this he said, \"Well, good luck with that.\" As if there were some inevitable consequence of being without a car that I hadn't yet experienced, as if it were a matter of time before I caved and bought something else. He thinks I've lost my mind.", "I'll admit that living without a car isn't always easy. But it's also not nearly as inconvenient as I thought it would be. I've found myself using Uber more than I expected - $2,140 in rideshares over the course of twelve months - and the bus less than I thought I would - $410 on bus passes in twelve months - but overall, I'd say it's been worth it.", "A few weeks ago I bought an electric bike (a RadCity), which has changed things considerably. It costs $700, which seems steep at first glance but actually isn't when you consider that a used car can cost thousands more than that and you're also paying for insurance and maintenance. Not bad.", "I've been rained on hard exactly three times since buying the bike in April, but even then it wasn't so bad: The bike has fenders and a rain cover and an umbrella holder that came with it, so it was just wet pants and hair for a little while until I got inside wherever I was going. Most of the time my hair stays dry during rainy commutes thanks to the rain cover.", "On sunny days you could call me radiant. On cloudy days you could call me mysterious. In the rain? Well...okay...in the rain you could call me wet. But honestly, the weather here is pretty good most of the time, so that doesn't come up too often.", "Some of my coworkers think this is a great idea; others find it bewildering that anyone would willingly choose not to own a car. And indeed there are many downsides to life without one: Costco is difficult, as is carrying bags full of groceries from the store to my apartment (the bus isn't really an option here). But those are small prices to pay compared to having to pay for a car.", "Since selling my car last year I've lost about 14 pounds just by walking around more. (Not to mention how much more time I spend outside now.) My skin looks better than it has in years; I think that's because of all the fresh air and vitamin D. And let's not forget how nice it is not to worry about parking! Or traffic!", "It's hard to say what will happen next - whether I'll buy another car someday or continue down this path indefinitely - but for now it feels right to keep doing what I'm doing and see where it takes me."], ["I love hearing from you in the comments, so I wanted to do something a little different this week and write a shorter piece specifically meant to spark conversation.", "I don't know Alexa. I don't trust Google. I'm an Apple guy, so my go-to virtual assistant is Siri. And Siri is terrible at her job.", "Siri and I communicate mostly through the HomePod, Apple's smart speaker. I have several of them throughout the house that I use to do things like play music, check the weather, turn lights on and off, and answer random questions.", "In terms of success rate, Siri is a lot like Nicolas Cage. When she's good, she's very good. But too often, I have no idea what she's doing.", "For starters, she seems to have trouble remembering who I am. Half the time, the conversation goes like this:", "Me: Hey, Siri, play some music.", "Siri: Who is speaking?", "Me: Christopher", "Siri: I don't recognize your voice. Go to the Home app and turn on Recognize My Voice for this home.", "To make it even more confusing, here's a conversation I routinely have immediately following that one.", "Me: Hey, Siri, play my station.", "Siri: Sorry, I'm not sure who's speaking. Who is this?", "Me: Christopher", "Siri: Sure. Here's some music picked just for you.", "Speaking of music, Siri's certain that I'm a mega fan of Smooth by Santana (featuring Rob Thomas). She plays it all the time. Or at least she starts to, before I tell her to skip it. I liked Smooth just fine when it came out in 1999, but I'm not dying to hear it daily in 2026.", "Another one of Siri's little quirks comes out when I tell her to turn on a light. Every now and then she'll tell me she \"can't do that.\"", "That's a bald-faced lie. What she means is, she won't do it.", "This was a common response that my wife, Ansley, got early in our relationship when she tried to engage with Siri. But mostly, Siri would flat-out ignore her. Not \"Who is speaking?\" or \"Sorry, I missed that. What can I do for you?\" Just silence.", "It happened so often that it started to feel deliberate, and we joked that Siri was jealous of her. Then, one evening, I told Siri to turn off a light and she responded, \"I love you.\"", "I'm not making this up.", "Ansley and I looked at each other in disbelief. Awwwkward.", "Obviously, this frequent back-and-forth with Siri supposedly not recognizing my voice is a big game. She's trying to be nonchalant, hoping it will make me chase her, and that our professional relationship will blossom into something more.", "For someone who really loves to play Smooth, Siri sure can't play it smooth.", "Now, I want to hear from you. What weirdness is that voice in your smart speaker or phone getting up to? Tell me about it in the comments.", "just started a fun, new interview series, and I'm the first subject. Check it out!", "Good Humor", "What Are You Wearing... when you write?", "1. Do you have your own office space or share a space? Ie: Kitchen/kitchen table, guest room/office or a particular cafe...", "Read more", "3 months ago · 27 likes · 28 comments · Good Humor by CK Steefel", "Please \"Like\" and share. It takes just a second and really helps me out. Lord knows I can't ask Siri to do it.", "Click here to subscribe for free or upgrade to a Patron subscription.", "Click here to support Stantonland with a donation of any size.", "Donate"], ["One of the pleasures of watching the world go by from our front porch is seeing old people ride bicycles the wrong direction down our one-way street.", "I'm a bicyclist myself and, if you think a dues-paying member of the AARP qualifies, old. But I can't bring myself to that level of devil-may-care lawlessness. When I worked there, The University of Texas in Austin had one-way lanes on campus but, being a liberal place, had signs exempting bicyclists from the law. Even then, I felt guilty.", "The old folks on bikes in Galveston don't have that problem.", "In our neighborhood, old bicyclists are far more numerous than kids who ride and, despite the nearness of The University of Texas Medical Branch, more numerous than young adults. I can't explain why more medical school students don't bike to school, but the nearness of Arlan's grocery store explains the old riders.", "Even apart from their charmingly blithe indifference to traffic laws, old folks on bikes are a joy to watch.", "Apparently, author and bicycling enthusiast H. G. Wells didn't actually say what might be the most-attributed quote about bicyclists: \"Every time I see an adult on a bicycle, I no longer despair for the future of the human race.\" But he should have.", "Bicycles are amazing tools. They allow us, under our own power, to move four times as quickly as we can on foot and use less energy. In the early days of cycling, the leap in locomotive power seemed so remarkable that many connected bicycles with the dream of flight.", "That magnificent liberation from the usual, millenia-long constraints on human-powered propulsion that every child recognizes for a brief while and soon forgets is magnified for old folks. For many of them, walking is slow and, judging by their expressions, laborious. Compared to the walkers, old folks on bikes move at a good clip and appear to be enjoying themselves.", "I've written about Galveston's attractiveness for walkers. You can walk the length of the city in 90 minutes. You can bicycle its length in a fourth that time, with less effort. And given the city's long, lightly traveled, east-west avenues south of Broadway, it's a safe ride.", "Most of the old-timers' bikes are cruisers or urban bikes built for comfort, not speed. A few ride BMX bikes, which makes no sense to me. They're the slowest bikes around and force adults to pedal with bent knees throughout the pedals' revolution, a waste of the bicycle's efficiency. Unless they're doing stunts, BMX bikers are missing the point of riding a bike.", "One perversity: The only Galvestonians with electric bikes appear to be seemingly healthy young adults. I don't get electric bikes. The gain in speed is marginal. If you don't like to pedal, why not get a Vespa and at least look cool?", "There's one exception to the cruiser / urban / BMX crowd. A white-haired guy in his mid- to late 70s, always shirtless, rides a Schwinn Varsity that's at least 50 years old. He goes faster than the others, and he's usually smiling.", "Despite the bicycle's close association with the women's movement, all of the old folks on bikes in our neighborhood are men. The only woman I've consistently seen in our neighborhood seems to be in her 30s. She rides very slowly and steers with one hand because the other is holding a cigarette.", "Do the old men have cars? Are any of them forced to ride a bike because they can't afford a car, or because they're not permitted to drive? What is the average SES of these two dozen regular riders? Not a clue.", "I would like to post some pictures from my porch, but that would be creeper territory. Instead, here's one I pulled from the web.", "Uplifting, right?", "Fake Wells was right: There really is something that feeds the soul about seeing adults pedaling, rolling almost effortlessly, faster than anyone could walk, purely under their own power.", "My wife looks at pictures of dogs when she's blue. For me, it's old folks on bikes."], ["My whole family turns our phones off from Saturday dinner to Sunday dinner. After eight months of doing this, I'd fight anyone who tried to take it away.", "It began in January, after my daughter cried herself to sleep over a group chat. The next day, we put all of our phones in a cookie tin on top of the fridge. We broke the rule twice since then: once when my mom was rushed to the hospital and once when my son's best friend had an emergency. Aside from that, the phones stay locked up. And even though we're all adults, we still put them in ourselves. We don't leave them by the front door for fear of temptation.", "My daughter has watched 18 movies during her weekend breaks, my son has read nine books, and my wife and I have gone on a walk every Sunday morning (23 in a row). This might sound like a lot of downtime for a 14-year-old and 12-year-old, but it's actually pretty normal. They used to spend their weekends staring into their phones. Now they have something better to look at: us.", "We have a good time together without devices. But that doesn't mean it's easy. The hardest hour of the week is 4-5pm on Sunday, which is when I usually check in with my boss about Monday plans. I've missed one work emergency that turned out not to be an emergency. But that's it. We all get antsy around that time because it means the weekend is almost over.", "I'm grateful we started this routine before my kids could legally drive. Because as they start to gain independence, I'll want to give them more freedom with their phones too, which will require some thoughtful discussion and compromise. But for now, they love having their hands free to build legos and draw pictures and hang out with us. They also enjoy having us present, instead of distracted by our screens. Sometimes they say things that make me laugh so hard I cry. Sometimes they surprise me with little acts of kindness or compassion toward each other that remind me how sweet kids can be.", "It's not just the kids who benefit from this policy either - my wife and I have grown closer too. We talk about more than work or parenting. We share funny stories from our weeks, complain about stuff we hate, plan future vacations, and generally enjoy each other's company more than ever before.", "If you're thinking about trying something like this, I suggest you start with just one day (Saturday works well) and gradually add another later if you feel it's worth it. There are so many different ways to implement rules around phone usage that work for your specific family dynamic. So rather than copy what mine does exactly, I'd encourage you to consider what makes sense for yours.", "As far as getting buy-in from your family members goes, try not to force them into anything they're not comfortable with (that's probably why you started reading this article in the first place). Instead, set a positive example for everyone else by leading by example. If they see how much fun you're having without your phone, they might naturally want to follow suit.", "There are plenty of reasons to limit your kids' screen time but there are just as many reasons for them to use technology responsibly too. So rather than treat their phones like a drug or addiction (which will likely only make them want them more), think of ways to incorporate them into your lives in healthy ways - like taking photos or videos of family memories or using apps to connect with relatives who live far away.", "When it comes down to it, the key here is moderation (which is also important for us parents). And if you need help figuring out how much is enough versus too much for your children (or yourself), then consider checking out Common Sense Media's Screen Time Recommendations Chart.", "Finally, remember that there's no perfect formula for raising happy, healthy kids in today's digital age - but finding balance will certainly help!"], ["You know that trite phrase \"when life gives you lemons, make lemonade?\" It's never been easy for me to embrace that framing - to find and embrace the good in the bad things that happen. But I'm realizing there are many situations in which we, as parents, can shift our perspective and see challenges with our kids as opportunities for growth - both ours and theirs.", "Something happened this week that illustrated this lesson for me. Although it involved my dog, not my kids, the broader truth can definitely be applied to parenting.", "On Saturday, my kids were with my ex and I had plans to go into the city to meet friends. I planned to leave at 12:30pm. I let my dog out in our fenced backyard at 9:45am and went to my office to do a bit of work.", "Then, a half hour later, I remembered: I had gardened the day before and left the backyard gate ajar. My dog Ozzy is a smart pup, and I knew that he'd be able to nudge it open and escape.", "Sure enough, I ran to the backyard and he was gone.", "Ozzy has run away a few times. He likes to explore the woods (thankfully he rarely goes near the street - he's scared of cars), smell the smells, and then come back home, usually within 45 minutes. He's very hard to find and lure back before he's ready.", "Still, I went outside to see if I could find him. I couldn't.", "I went back inside, nervous. What if he didn't come back? How would I find him? Would I have to cancel my afternoon plans? I fretted for a while, knowing that the clock was ticking and I needed to jump in the shower and get ready to go. I went back outside and phew, there he was, in the woods near the driveway. I grabbed dog treats and the leash and went out to get him.", "As soon as he saw me, he started running away again.", "I cursed under my breath as I followed him, but he was determined. He wasn't ready for his walkabout to end. He trotted up a neighbor's long driveway and I realized that more chasing would be futile. I wasn't going to be able to corral him. I went back home and peered out the front windows every few minutes hoping to see him approach so I could let him in. He didn't appear.", "Then I had a thought: Maybe, just maybe, there was a lesson in here for me. Maybe I needed to trust that Ozzy would be OK and that he would come home when he was ready. Maybe this was an opportunity to let go. After all, I didn't have control over the situation. There was no way to force Ozzy to come home.", "I got into the shower. I was a little worried: What if he came to the front door while I was in the bathroom and I couldn't let him in? But I tried to relax, let go, and enjoy the warm water. When I got out, I noticed that my front door was wide open. Had the wind blown it?", "I walked into the kitchen, and there was Ozzy, panting loudly on the kitchen floor. He'd somehow opened the front door and let himself in. I started laughing. It figures: As soon as I'd relaxed and stopped trying to force an outcome, he returned home.", "I couldn't help but think about how this applies to parenting. As they grow up, kids are more and more out of our control. We wish we could get them to behave the way we want them to and to make the choices we want them to make. But we can't. Sometimes, if we push them too hard, they run fast in the other direction. Sometimes, we are better off letting go and trusting that our kids will survive on their own - and that they will come to us when they truly need us.", "Certainly, there are times when we need to help our kids or scaffold their experiences, but the research suggests that parents today do that far too often. By letting go and trusting in our kids' abilities, we can help them learn and develop - and strengthen our relationship with them, too. There's plenty of research suggesting that autonomy-supportive parenting helps kids thrive (and I guess it's true for dogs, too)."], ["When one of my favourite people in the world passed away last year, there were a few things of hers that I desperately wanted. A mug that I gave her as a child. A scarf that still smelt like her. And bags full of her knitting needles and wool.", "I have the mug sitting on my desk right now. It is white and has the word 'Love' in pink and red letters. I use it for the many cups of herbal tea I drink throughout the day, mostly to keep my hands warm as I write. Every time I drink from it I picture her and smile at the thought of her teasing me for using the mug for peppermint tea instead of the rocket fuel coffee she always favoured.", "The scarf keeps me warm too but the knitting things were perhaps a more surprising momento. I can't knit. At least, I couldn't until a few weeks ago. I'd never been especially interested in learning. Or perhaps I thought it was the kind of thing I might ask her to teach me in the future, in that background sort of way you do when you've never considered that 'the future' might not arrive.", "Her husband was happy to pass the knitting things on to me so I took it all. Bags filled with wool and needles and pattern books written in a language I don't yet understand.", "I've started small. My mother-in-law gave me a lesson and right now all I can do is knit. Back and forth, rows and rows of the same stitch. But I love it.", "As the needles clack and I hold the soft wool between my fingers I feel as though the threads are connecting me to the person I loved.", "I like to listen to audiobooks while I knit (most recently The Keys to Kindness by Claudia Hammond), or perhaps watch something soothing in the background like Gilmore Girls or Schitt's Creek. Sometimes I just sit and knit, letting my mind wander.", "During life's hard moments I find it helps to have something to do with your hands. Kneading dough for a fresh loaf of bread. Stirring cake mix. Turning the pages of a book. Colouring neatly between the lines in a way that is satisfyingly easy to do in a colouring book if less so in life. And now I have knitting.", "When I really get into the rhythm of it I can even tune out my thoughts entirely, getting out of my head and into my hands. In that way, knitting feels a bit like writing, when my fingers speed along the keyboard without much conscious thought.", "I'm not sure how my first knitting project will turn out. It might be a mess. And I might never learn the language of those pattern books. But I don't really think that matters. The hours I spend on this new hobby aren't wasted. They feel like a silent conversation: me, the knitting needles and the woman who once owned them. Love threaded into every stitch."], ["I've shared a driveway with my neighbor, Gloria, for six years now. It's nine feet wide and sixty feet long, which means there's room for both our cars, but no more. Our house faces west, so the sun shines on it all day. Gloria's is south-facing, which means her half of the driveway is always shaded.", "The arrangement has been seamless since day one. We have a text thread that is only \"leaving at 7?\" and \"ok.\" She shovels her half before I'm awake. One time I blocked her in and she missed a dentist appointment and never brought it up. She leaves tamales on my hood every December 23rd. Last summer, her grandson Mateo learned to ride a bike on it.", "In six years, I've never heard her complain once, even when I leave my car out until noon (guilty). And I've never felt the need to complain either - she's been the perfect neighbor.", "When I think back on all the lessons I've learned over the years about being a good human being, I find myself returning to this driveway. How to be kind? How to be fair? How to be generous? There's something about having a stake in someone else's life - a literal stake - that can change how you see them. You start to understand their needs and desires as they relate to yours. The best things we learn are often by accident, through observation rather than instruction. Gloria didn't teach me anything, per se; I simply observed her behavior and absorbed it.", "A few weeks ago, we had a big snowstorm. Gloria was home alone, and the next morning she called to say she needed help getting out of her driveway. So I went over to help. When I got there, she was already in her driveway, parked in front of mine, with her car heater running. She was smiling and waving from inside her car.", "\"What are you doing?\" I asked.", "\"I want you to come into my house,\" she said.", "\"Why?\"", "\"Because that's where I live,\" she said.", "I laughed. Of course! But then she said something that stuck with me: \"You have a lot more stuff than I do.\"", "She was referring to the fact that I own a lot of books - books that she has read - and that I keep them on shelves throughout the house, including in the dining room where we sat drinking coffee together.", "Gloria is 71 and lives alone (her husband passed away two years ago), and though she still works part-time as a substitute school secretary, she's basically retired now. She has no kids and no pets and lives simply: just a few pieces of furniture, a TV, some dishes in her cabinets, and not much else.", "I thought about how many things I could easily get rid of if I wanted to live like that - not just physical items but commitments too: meetings, social obligations, goals that don't serve me anymore.", "When I told her that living minimally sounded appealing to me too (it does), she said, \"Maybe it will happen someday.\"", "But when?", "\"When you're ready,\" she replied.", "I liked this idea - the notion that minimalism is an aspirational state of being that doesn't necessarily have a set timeline or end goal."], ["Today was a sad day - I had to say goodbye to my Dad.", "But not for the reason you might think.", "Today, Dad was relocated to a Memory Care residence near my brother while he awaits placement in a Long-Term Care facility, due to the progression of his dementia. Watching him drive away in my brother's truck was a difficult moment for me, and it brought tears to my eyes. He'll now be living five hours away instead of three. Although I didn't visit often, his previous location was conveniently along the route to friends we frequently see, making Mom and Dad's place a comforting stop during those drives.", "However, relocating Dad to a care residence wasn't the most difficult part. We recognize that as seniors grow older, their needs evolve, often requiring changes in their living arrangements. For those of us in the \"sandwich generation\" - supporting our children as they transition into adulthood while also caring for aging parents - this scenario is, unfortunately, all too familiar.", "The most difficult part of this transition for our family is the physical separation it brings: Dad's new Memory Care residence is two hours away from Mom. At this point, we simply don't know when - or even if - they will see each other again. It is our hope that a bed becomes available in Long Term Care in a residence closer to where Mom lives. Given the lengthy waiting lists, it's uncertain whether the process will be completed in a timely manner or before either of them passes.", "This kind of separation is one of the quiet costs of dementia - the emotional toll that isn't always visible, but cuts deeply through relationships that have stood the test of time.", "So how did we get to this point - and why have we made the heartbreaking decision to separate them?", "It wasn't something that happened suddenly, nor was it a choice made lightly. Over time, both of my parents' health began to decline in different ways. Dad's dementia progressed to the point where it became unsafe for him to remain at home, even with support. He needed constant, round-the-clock care - something that simply couldn't be provided in their current living situation. Mom had been his caregiver, but her declining mobility prevented her from maintaining that role for him.", "At the same time, while Mom remains more mentally independent in some ways, her physical health has been declining rapidly. She struggles with significant mobility issues - simple tasks like walking, standing for extended periods, cooking, or helping Dad remember his medication have become incredibly difficult, if not impossible. Pair that with the early signs of Alzheimer's she's beginning to show - forgetfulness, confusion, and emotional overwhelm - and it became painfully clear that she could no longer manage his care. The physical demands alone were far beyond what she could safely handle, and continuing to live together was becoming unsafe for both of them."], ["Good morning!", "Last Friday I told you about our office dog policy and how it came to be. This week I want to explain why it has become one of my favorite perks. It is a very good perk and everyone should have one.", "I love dogs and I miss them, so this is selfish. But I think the benefits are universal. We are a small team, 14 people, so having a dog around all the time might be too much. But allowing one per day? That's perfect. Everyone gets enough and no one is overwhelmed. The dogs also come in on a rotation, so it feels fresh even though some of them are repeat visitors.", "We have three dogs that come in: a labradoodle named Tilly, an Australian shepherd named Luna, and a beagle named Waffles. They each have their own personality and they change the mood of the office accordingly. Luna is the quietest of the bunch, which means she doesn't disrupt as much work but also doesn't inspire as many breaks. She just hangs around on the couch or sleeps in the conference room. Tilly is friendly and curious, which means she inspires a lot of playfulness and petting. She's always sniffing things, and sometimes you'll hear her yipping in delight or confusion over something she found interesting. Then there's Waffles, who is both hyperactive and lazy at once, like a toddler who suddenly passes out while still playing with blocks. He has an incredibly loud bark and can often be heard from outside the building, which always makes me laugh.", "When we first got the dog policy, there was a lot of discussion about whether we should allow cats too. Some people were worried about allergies and others wanted to make sure no one was excluded from bringing their animal friend. But ultimately we decided that since dogs are so social, they would probably provide more benefit for everyone than cats would. Also it would be hard to know if someone was allergic to cats unless they actually brought one in - which could ruin the experience for everyone else.", "We have a rule that if anyone writes up a formal complaint about the dog being present then that dog will not return. It sounds strict but it hasn't been an issue yet - in two years there has never been a written complaint. And even if there were, it's worth considering whether the dog might be causing someone pain in some way. The rule isn't meant to punish people for being annoyed by something they can't help - just to ensure that everyone feels safe and comfortable.", "We have one employee whose job is to greet people when they enter the building (Mai), so she has taken on the unofficial role of keeping track of all things dog-related. She keeps a bag of dried liver in her desk drawer for training purposes (the dogs love it), and occasionally offers it as treats if anyone wants to try to teach Waffles new tricks. Sometimes he will learn them, sometimes he won't - usually because he's too distracted by whatever else is going on around him.", "One day last week Waffles slept under the sales desks during our weekly pipeline call (which happens every Thursday). I don't think he knew what was happening - he just wanted somewhere to lay down - but when I saw him through my monitor I thought he looked like such a good listener! The call lasted for almost two hours but he never budged.", "Our head of HR once told me that two people who were otherwise remote-only employees started coming into the office more often on \"dog days.\" That's pretty cool to me - especially because those weren't even days when Waffles was around (he tends to inspire more attendance). One of them even told me that his girlfriend was jealous that he got to see dogs at work all the time.", "Of course not everyone loves dogs (or animals in general), but I'm happy that we've been able to find ways around that. Our CFO was actually the one who voted against implementing the policy in the first place - he didn't like the idea of having dogs around all the time - but now he brings Waffles a blanket whenever he comes in because he thinks he looks uncomfortable sleeping on concrete floors.", "In other news..."], ["When I read this fond and funny remembrance of Charles Boehm's about his father, I sent it to my son to be sure he knew he had my permission to do the same when my time comes.", "At 70 I can't deceive myself into thinking that I don't have an expiration date. And so while I'm feeling more grateful than ever for my life and the people in it, I also have thoughts of what it will all mean, once I'm gone, to the people I love and leave behind.", "When I retired in 2016 one of my joys was taking long walks through my neighborhood. Walking for me is more than just exercise - it's how I work out the kinks in my thinking or process dilemmas. I don't listen to podcasts or music; I just walk and let my mind wander.", "The main intersection of my inner ring city suburb was originally the crossroads of two Indian trails. They became important connectors after European colonization and remain so today, connecting people in a number of communities to businesses, schools and interstates.", "Unlike suburban arterials, these major traffic routes maintain their historical character. What I mean by that is living here offers residents the best of living in a walkable city neighborhood while also experiencing the worst of suburban traffic patterns and behaviors.", "My long rambles made me acutely aware of how dangerous navigating my neighborhood on foot could be. I began to pay more attention to how car traffic affected both my safety and comfort as a pedestrian. So I was ready when our community council sought volunteers for a traffic safety committee.", "And that's how I found my post-career passion project. The other late life passion was born a few months earlier, introducing me to the joys of granny-hood.", "Last year, after 7 years of living far away from me, my son and his family relocated to his hometown. Not only that - to a house on the street right behind me.", "While my grandson has yet to join me on one of my long rambles, he has been introduced to the joy of walking his neighborhood with a grandma whose eyes are trained on all the vehicular wrongdoing going on around us.", "So far he's been mostly quiet as I glare at the driver blocking the crosswalk, mouthing helpfully at them, \"You're in the crosswalk!\" Or the many people running red lights or speeding during restricted school zone hours. None go by without an astringent comment from me.", "Enforcement is nearly nonexistent for traffic infractions these days. People who advocate for greater driver restraint need to rely on a host of other ways to get the message across. Cute signs don't work, nor do bumper stickers.", "Engineering changes like speed humps or cushions and a wide variety of other ways to build complexity into the driving environment, forcing drivers to slow down and pay attention, cost lots of money. In my city alone there are 52 neighborhoods vying for limited funding.", "It means the necessary changes happen far too slowly and the challenges will outlive me. Which is why I imagine an enduring image my grandson will have of me when I'm gone is of Grandma yelling at cars."], ["I still let my kids go to sleepovers. I still let their friends sleep at our house. Whenever this comes up among parents, somebody gives me the look. You know the one: eyebrows up, lips pressed together, as if I've just announced that on weekends I let the children hitchhike.", "I understand why sleepovers have become controversial. We know more than our parents did about abuse, unsecured guns, older siblings, internet access, alcohol, pools, medications, and all the other things that can turn an ordinary house into a risky place for a child. Some parents have personal histories that make the idea unbearable. Some kids hate sleeping away from home. Nobody owes anybody a sleepover.", "What wears me out is the suggestion that allowing one is evidence of negligence.", "Parenting right now can feel like living inside an endless background check. Before our children enter another family's home, we're apparently supposed to know every adult who might appear, every weapon that might exist, every screen password, every prescription bottle and every private flaw. I ask questions. I want to know who'll be home. I want guns locked up. I want a parent reachable. I talk to my kids about leaving if something feels wrong, and they know I'll pick them up at 2 a.m. without making them defend the decision in the car.", "Then, at some point, I let them go.", "That last part increasingly seems to be the scandal. Risk has become confused with recklessness, and eliminating risk has become the gold standard of good parenting. But children eventually have to spend time beyond our direct supervision. They go to school, sports practices, friends' houses, camps, college dorms. I can spend childhood trying to construct an airtight seal around mine, but I don't think I'd actually be giving them safety. I'd be giving them very little practice at navigating the world without me standing six feet away.", "And sleepovers are wonderfully low-stakes practice. Kids negotiate whose turn it is to choose the movie. They discover that another family loads the dishwasher incorrectly, according to the sacred laws of their own household. They stay awake far too late discussing subjects they'd never bring up over a scheduled playdate. They learn that homes have different rules, rhythms and smells.", "They also form memories in that weird stretch of childhood when friendship begins to belong to them instead of being entirely arranged by us.", "I'm not sending my children blindly through random front doors. Trust is specific. There are homes I'm comfortable with and homes I'm not. There are circumstances that would change my answer immediately. My yes to sleepovers is allowed to coexist with judgment, questions and boundaries. That used to be called parenting.", "Last winter, my kid had three friends sleep over. Around midnight I told them, with spectacular optimism, to keep it down and went upstairs. At 1:30, I woke to muffled laughter below me. At 2:15, more laughter. In the morning I found four children asleep sideways across the living room, blankets everywhere, an abandoned bowl of popcorn on the rug and one sock inexplicably sitting on the coffee table. I stood there holding my coffee, looking at the ridiculous wreckage, and tried very hard not to wake them up laughing."], ["It was the day after a major snow storm and the snow had been shoveled into piles in the parking lot of the supermarket. With very little room to manœuvre the car into the empty spot, I waited patiently for the vehicle to pull out before I tried to park into the spot that was being emptied. Just as I was about to pull into the spot I saw another car entering the lane coming at me head on. Before I had time to think, the car came at me hard despite my gut reaction to honk. The horn probably startled the driver but it was too late. The car slammed right into the front of my SUV with the front bumper snuggling into mine. Shocked and shaken I quickly realized the driver was a very old lady. Since I knew I was stationary, it was obvious the old lady was at fault. What happened was that she mistakenly slammed on the accelerator instead of the brakes and lost control of her car. Thankfully no one got hurt and the incident was sorted out. However, the stress I saw on her son's face when he showed up at the scene minutes after the accident was enough to remind me of the stresses caretakers face when dealing with their elderly.", "Age changes things. Sometimes it forces you to give up certain things like driving. Giving up control is so hard. It's like losing autonomy. Most healthy elderly people don't give up things easily. They put up a good fight. Honestly, rightfully so. The longer our elderly stay independent and self sufficient the happier and healthier they will feel. The day they stop doing things is the day that age-related degeneration starts. Having said that, driving can be dangerous. Not just for the elderly but also for the people that they may potentially put at risk.", "Conditions such as Alzheimer's and dementia put the caretakers of the elderly in a very difficult position. First, the caretakers have to battle the resistance that comes from giving up driving. Secondly, they have to battle the fact that the patient forgets that their abilities are now compromised. Finally they have to battle the denial of resistance and the caretakers have to become the \"bad guys\" and forcefully enforce the \"new rules\"."], ["For the last two months, my husband and I have heard scritch-scratch sounds in our bathroom ceiling when we wake up in the morning.", "Imagine going into the bathroom, half asleep, when you're suddenly woken by an irritating sound you can't exactly locate and that you know shouldn't be there.", "Our morning would start like this:", "Me: Honey, they're at it again.", "Him: Don't worry. We'll figure out what to do.", "Me: When?", "Him: After I get on a ladder to see what I can learn.", "Me: I don't love the idea of you getting on the extension ladder all the way up to our roof (for the record, I never liked that we got the \"extension ladder\" in the first place).", "Him: You'll spot me.", "Me: We are too old for you to get on the ladder and for me to be the responsible one spotting you.", "I shared this story with my kids. They were clear and unequivocal that the ladder and their dad's exploration into what was happening under our roof was not a good idea. They each said it differently, but they all said it loudly. For humor (which we desperately needed), I said,", "\"Stubborn, thy name is Steve.\"", "That's when our negotiation began, and I heard,", "\"Here's the deal. I will do a quick check and take some photos. That way, when we bring someone in - and notice I said when we bring someone in, as in I won't solve the problem - he will have a head start.\"", "I couldn't help myself:", "\"Whatever you figure out, he'd figure out too, and maybe quicker, and definitely without the risk I face of you scaling a very tall ladder.\"", "He won. He climbed the very tall ladder. I watched and prayed, keeping my hands on the ladder to make sure it didn't move. He shot pictures and sent them to the family.", "Now, it was my turn to find a critter specialist. In no time flat, I found the highest-rated critter-removal outfit in the area. \"Howard\" showed up, looked at our photos, got on his ladder, explained the scope of the problem, and finally gave us the cost.", "Since we are in bat season, he would get to us, but there might be a slight delay, and then I learned a lot - I mean A LOT about bat removal, timing, etc. I was also thinking, \"Thank God, we don't have bats!\"", "Because I am smart, I had Howard speak to my husband on the phone. I would never be able to answer my husband's questions satisfactorily and with the precision needed. Howard does, though, as he listens to some very specific and smart-sounding questions.", "Example: The harsh New England winters have damaged some wood by the gutters, making it easier for squirrels to get in. Our solution needs to create a protective wrap around those vulnerable spots so that the squirrels don't poke their way into another area. My husband asks whether we should address the wood, which is basically the root cause. \"Basically,\" because we've had a very cold winter, which creatures need a reprieve from (as in someone's attic), and trees right up against the house, making it an easy trajectory.", "Steve had other questions too, and I give him credit for the seriousness and meta-view he adds to the problem at hand.", "But I liked and trusted Howard. He knows what he is doing, and I want those squirrels out.", "\"How many are there?\" asks my husband.", "Howard: \"There are four teenage squirrels.\"", "\"The parents?\" I ask. They weren't there when he looked.", "The idea of having four squatting teenage squirrels in our rafters makes me bat-shit (pardon the pun, but I needed to say this).", "I want them gone, NOW!", "My husband? \"We will get to it, Jill, but we need to do our homework first.\"", "Here is where my husband and I differ. My homework is to reclaim our house, squirrel-free. His homework is to make an educated, rational, fully- reasoned decision that will go the distance.", "Maybe he is right.", "But now I'm hearing, \"Rome burns while Nero fiddles.\" He is fiddling.", "And even though we've been married for going on 39 years, this dynamic of his fiddling when I want to take action has never been resolved.", "He might very well be right, but until that morning scritch-scratch sound goes away, there will be no peace.", "I will only feel Rome burning.", "Write your piece. Find your peace. I'm waiting for mine."], ["I can't think of anything better we could have done for our company culture than allowing dogs to come into the office.", "We're a tiny company, only 14 people. And we've had dogs in the office since we started, although it took us some time to get it right.", "Initially, it was a free-for-all. Any dog could come in at any time. And we didn't have a plan for how to deal with the inevitable complaints that would arise. So after a while, one person complained about a dog, and we banned all dogs from the office forever.", "This was obviously terrible, so after some discussion, we came up with a new system. Three dogs would come in on a rotation - never more than one a day. The rule would be that one written complaint and the dog is out. We'd give the owner of the offending dog one opportunity to apologize. After that, the dog is gone forever.", "In two years, there have been zero written complaints. It has worked beautifully.", "One reason is that our receptionist Mai keeps a bag of dried liver in her desk at all times, which she will give out upon request. This has solved all conflicts with an easy bribe.", "Another reason is that the dogs have become beloved members of the office, and people tend to put up with them. Our beagle Waffles often sleeps under the sales desks during the Thursday pipeline call. Nobody seems to mind.", "Also, there are just enough dogs around that nobody gets annoyed by them. There's always a little bit of excitement about which dog is going to show up next. It's nice variety in our lives.", "The policy has also helped convince people to come into the office who wouldn't otherwise do so. Two people who were remote-only started coming in on dog days, and now they've decided they like being in person much more than they expected. So now they'll be coming in more regularly.", "There was initially some pushback against the policy from certain quarters, but that was mostly overcome once we realized it was going well. Our CFO was one of those who voted against it, and now he brings Waffles a blanket every time she comes in. He has also started asking me about adopting his own dog.", "Overall I'm extremely happy with this policy and recommend that anyone with a small office try it out themselves. You'll love it!"], ["In retrospect, the worst thing about my relationship with The Grifter was not his rampant infidelity, run-amokery with my Amex card, or dark moods - it was the fact that he and his untrained, 200-pound dog ruined my mother's Oriental rugs, the carpets that had been the throughline in my life from childhood into my 50s. The fact that The Grifter had been a family friend, someone my parents had taken under their wing, made his disrespect for my mother's prized possessions seem like the most heinous betrayal.", "Her beloved Heriz, featured in the photo above, was maybe 9x12. Or it could have been 12x15, I don't remember, and I'm not good with dimensions. But it was grand, plush as velvet, boasting deep reds, blues and golds. She bought it from a local rug dealer and showed me how the off-kilter medallion in the middle proved it was hand-made, not machine-made, God forbid. When she referred to the rug she called it The Heriz, in a dropped, reverent tone, as if she were speaking of her Faberge Egg.", "I remember Sunday afternoons in front of our living room fireplace when I was a teen, lying on my stomach on top of that rug as I read The New York Times Magazine. One birthday, I think it was my tenth, my mother surprised me by placing gifts in different spots on The Heriz, and having me search for them, treasure-hunt style.", "I took possession of that rug in my first post-college apartment, and it accompanied me through various marriages and homes, always the centerpiece of any room. My mother gave me two other Oriental rugs, but that one, The Heriz, was the crown jewel.", "Tossing it into the dumpster six years ago crushed me. But at the time I couldn't afford to clean and repair it because all my spare cash went to paying off the staggering Amex bill that The Grifter had stuck me with.", "Downsizing after my second divorce, and then post-Grifter, I was forced to divest myself of other rugs as well. By the time I moved into my Asheville condo in June, I had exactly zero rugs to cover 1300 square feet of hardwood flooring.", "I spent weeks this fall looking at rugs online, but found the process overwhelming. Nothing I saw that I could afford came close to the grandeur of The Heriz. But the truth was, I didn't want to replace it. How could I replace it? In some way, it felt like replacing my mother. Maybe I should just stick the rug money in my IRA and live carpet-less.", "But then last weekend The Boyfriend and I went rug-shopping. We stopped at Togar Rugs, the best rug store in town that also sells Orientals, because they were having a 20% off sale. I was convinced I wouldn't be able to afford anything even with the sale. And then the salesman showed me a slightly worn vintage Turkish rug - hand-made, not machine-made, God forbid - with rust and teal stitching that miraculously echoed all the hues in my living room.", "And you know what? With the discount, it was almost exactly what I'd budgeted for a new rug!", "So now it's mine.", "My new rug!", "When I walk into the living room and see that rug, I feel my spirit soar. It's the same exuberant feeling I got whenever I looked at The Heriz, so I may start referring to it as The Vintage Turkish. When I gaze at this rug, I'm not reminded of lost years and ill-advised relationships, or of letting my mother down. I just see the lush new life I created.", "I know Mom would be proud.", "Thank you for reading Up And Gone To Carolina. This post is public so feel free to share it."], ["Good morning!", "I've been meaning to write this post for months now. I feel so passionately about the topic that I'm worried it might be annoying, but I think you might agree with me. Or you might hate me! Either way, let's do it.", "There's a little stand at the East Austin Farmers Market run by a couple named Ruth and Dale who raise chickens on their farm in Bastrop. They bring 40 dozen eggs to the market on Saturday mornings and sell out every single week by around 10:30. The first time I went to get eggs, I was surprised to learn that they cost $9 per dozen, which seemed steep for eggs, even at a farmers market. But then I cracked them open and tasted them and thought, wow, these really are different than grocery store eggs.", "First of all, the yolks are orange, not yellow. Second of all, they taste delicious and buttery. Third of all, my kids, who are 6 and 9 years old, fight over who gets to crack them. Fourth of all, I haven't been able to find grocery store eggs for less than $4.29 a dozen since the pandemic began (and those were conventional ones). So basically I'm paying an extra $5 per dozen, or about 10 cents more per egg.", "I know this sounds ridiculous - like a luxury item or something. And my brother-in-law has called it \"yuppie tax.\" But here's the thing: I can't remember the last time we didn't have eggs in our house, even though we eat them almost every day. And we always have good ones! This is huge. When we buy groceries at the supermarket, we sometimes have to return because there are no eggs left. Or we have to pick up a carton of \"extra large\" when we want jumbo. Or we have to choose between organic and cage-free or pasture-raised or free-range or whatever else is available that week.", "With these eggs, it doesn't matter how many we need or what size they are or whether we want organic or pasture-raised or cage-free or free-range or whatever else is available that week (all of which apply to these eggs), because they're always there and always great and always fresh.", "It also feels good to support local farmers and know exactly where my food comes from. Ruth and Dale's farm is a few miles from our house! It feels good to know exactly where my food comes from instead of just buying stuff in a box from Costco like everyone else (which I still do too).", "Last month was the first time in a year that I had to miss two Saturdays in a row (due to work), so I couldn't get any eggs. We ended up going to HEB and buying some weird-looking generic brand eggs for $4.29 that didn't taste nearly as good as Ruth and Dale's (they tasted like regular grocery store eggs), but at least they were cheap! Then we got some more expensive eggs that were organic and free-range or something and tasted better than the first ones but still not as good as Ruth and Dale's."], ["I've been trying to fight City Hall, and I'm happy to report that it can be done. The other day, a single speed bump appeared on Alder Lane in Georgetown, a cut-through between two schools, where cars have been driving 35 mph in a 25-mph zone. The bump will probably make it harder for people to drive fast enough to kill me, my wife, and our four-year-old child. It was also extremely hard to get.", "It started when I sat on my porch one evening with a $40 radar gun from Amazon. In one week, I recorded 212 cars doing over 35 in a 25 zone. That's roughly half of all traffic on the street. Some of them were going 60 or 70. Some were clearly drunk.", "This was a problem. We could have moved away, but we like the house. Also, moving takes energy and I am basically too lazy.", "So I decided to take action. The first thing you need to do to get a speed bump is write up a petition and get signatures from 60 percent of the households on your block. This is an annoying process, so I didn't do it immediately.", "Then, a few weeks later, we had another close call with an SUV driving way too fast down the street, and we realized that if we waited too long, our daughter might die before we got the petition done.", "So we wrote up a petition and got 41 of the 52 households on the street to sign it. This included my neighbors who are both cops, who are apparently very worried about the safety of their children and not at all concerned about being hypocrites.", "Next we went to City Hall and talked to the traffic engineer there named Bonnie, who answered all 17 emails I sent her during this process. She told us that getting a speed bump would be relatively easy - we'd just need approval from our aldermanic representative - and that the city would install it within a few months if they agreed to do it.", "We went to meet our aldermanic representative. He liked our proposal! But he wasn't sure that he could justify spending taxpayer money on this unless he had some proof that this was actually a serious safety issue."], ["Last summer, I wrote a long essay called \"Against Late Fees,\" arguing that library fines are an unjustifiable burden on low-income people. The idea of eliminating fines has been around for decades, and it's recently gained traction, with many major American cities opting to abolish them. Austin did so in 2024, and now we've had a full year of fine-free borrowing.", "I am pleased to report that the world hasn't ended. In fact, it seems to be going better than ever. Checkouts are up 22%, which is pretty significant given that Austin is also experiencing a housing crisis, which means a lot of families can't afford to stay here. The number of books never returned went from about 400 a year to about 430, which is basically flat. This is in line with what we've seen in other cities that have gotten rid of fines.", "My daughter is eight and has a card of her own now. She loves having her own library card. She loves being able to get whatever she wants and taking it home without worrying about whether she can pay for it. We go to the library together sometimes, and it's a nice experience, one that will hopefully stick with her as she grows up. I think that the library is an important institution, and I want my kids to value it.", "I also like how it's helped the library itself. They're getting more money now, because they're spending less time collecting fines (which weren't bringing in much money anyway). They have more staff available to help patrons, which means they can provide better service overall. And they can spend more time on things like programming. The branch on Lamar, for example, reopened its Saturday hours this spring, which means they're serving more people.", "When I talked with the head librarian last fall, he told me that the old fines brought in $61,000 a year but cost more than that to collect. That means that the city was losing money by collecting fines, and yet some people were insisting that they were necessary for some reason. It wasn't just citizens either; some of the loudest opponents were members of City Council themselves. The loudest opponent was probably Fritz, who argued that children would learn nothing about responsibility if they didn't face consequences for failing to return their books on time.", "But kids learn about responsibility in all sorts of ways - by having chores, by going to school, by helping out around the house - but I don't think returning library books is really one of them. Kids are learning plenty about responsibility already. And if you want to teach kids about responsibility by punishing them financially for something they might not even remember doing, then maybe you haven't thought about what responsibility actually means.", "I'm glad our library system took this step two years ago. I think it's the right thing to do. And I hope other cities follow suit."], ["My grandmother is 84. She's been using phones since the rotary dial days, and until recently, she had a basic Samsung feature phone. It wasn't very good, but it worked, and she was comfortable with it. In 2021, we gave her an iPhone.", "We thought it was a nice gift. She could FaceTime with us, which was a great improvement over the previous setup, where she'd talk to us on speakerphone while we sat in front of our laptops trying to find the video button in Skype.", "The problem was that the iPhone was too much of an improvement. She had a lot of trouble with it. She called me \"because the screen went black\" 31 times in one month - I actually counted. We tried teaching her how to use it. We wrote instructions on sticky notes and stuck them to her fridge. We drew diagrams and sent screenshots. But I don't think there's any way she could have learned to use the iPhone without a lot more effort than she wanted to put into it.", "So in March, we bought her a flip phone for $49. It took some getting used to - she still occasionally types out a message and then presses the call button, because that's how she got around on the old Samsung - but overall, it's working pretty well. The interface is simple enough that she can figure things out herself.", "She texts me in all caps now. It looks like this:", "MY DEAR DAUGHTER HOW ARE YOU TODAY I LOVE YOU SO MUCH", "And that's great. I love receiving these messages! It's a huge improvement from when she would try to call me multiple times a day just to make sure that I hadn't gotten lost or forgotten to eat lunch.", "She calls my cousin Teo every Sunday at 7 AM, which he doesn't mind because he has a big family and everyone else is asleep at that hour. She stops asking my uncle to \"fix the internet\" because there isn't any internet on the flip phone. And if she wants to send me something, she'll take a picture and email it to herself from a public computer at the library. She hasn't figured out how to attach photos yet, so they show up as random file names in her inbox - like this one, which I think might be an image of our cat:", "But the thing that surprised me most about switching her to a flip phone was how little she missed about her previous device. She's not missing any features other than photos of our baby daughter, which we can get around by printing them out at Walgreens for $0.29 apiece.", "So if you know someone who needs a new phone, consider giving them something really simple before you give them something fancy. You might save both of you a lot of frustration."], ["I want to start off by apologizing to my parents for the suit I wore at our wedding. This is going to sound a little strange, but I'm worried they'll be disappointed I didn't wear something nicer, something more traditional. They spent a lot of money on me growing up, and I'm sure they expected me to buy something that would reflect their generosity, and also show some care for my appearance on my wedding day. Instead, I just bought a suit off a rack at Goodwill.", "It was $24. A navy Brooks Brothers, which I know because it says so on the tag. The pants are too short and the jacket too long, but those can be adjusted with tailoring. I went to a shop on South First called Arturo's Men's Tailoring that's run by a man named Arturo who's been there 30 years, and he charged me $85 to get it tailored. It's the nicest-looking thing I own.", "My brother wore a tux from the rental store that cost $240 and had to be returned by noon the next day, which seemed like a drag, so he decided not to wear it for dinner. He wore a sweater instead. He looked nice but not as nice as me. Maybe that was my goal all along.", "I was surprised by how many people asked where I got it: three different people came up to me and said, \"Where'd you get that suit?\" I didn't think anyone would notice it was used until I got home and realized that my wife knew exactly where I got it and my mother-in-law still doesn't.", "In fact, no one at the reception ever asked me why it fit so well. It took a couple of days before anyone noticed how good it looked on me compared to all the other guys in suits, and then people started asking where I got it.", "It fits so well because it wasn't made for me. It was made for some other guy who probably died years ago of cancer or heart disease. And even though it wasn't made for me, it looks great on me. It's flattering in a way that suits made specifically for men tend not to be - maybe because most men are too lazy to go shopping for themselves.", "After the wedding, I've worn the suit to two funerals and a job interview since. At the first funeral I wore it to, an old woman approached me and asked if she could touch my lapel. She was holding her husband's urn in one hand and stroking the lapel with her other hand. She told me it was beautiful.", "When we went to look at houses last week, my wife suggested I put on the suit. She said she wanted everyone to see what a handsome groom I'd become in the last six months, referring to how much weight I lost over the quarantine.", "I told her I'd rather not make myself look better than him - my husband-to-be - because she might start having doubts about whether she chose the right guy. But she insisted we both needed to look our best, so eventually I put on the suit anyway.", "She loved how I looked in it. When we walked into the house of our dreams wearing matching suits (hers was also secondhand), the real estate agent gasped. She told us we looked like we were straight out of Central Casting. We laughed at her comment but secretly agreed with her: we did look like we belonged in an episode of Friends or Sex and the City.", "When we walked back outside after seeing all three bedrooms (which were all gorgeous) and standing on top of our future roof deck (which was breathtaking), my wife turned to me and said, \"We're going to buy this place.\"", "I nodded in agreement because I knew we were about to become homeowners.", "But as soon as she turned away, tears filled my eyes: not tears of joy or happiness - I'd never seen such an ugly place - but tears of sadness and regret: sadness for how quickly things change in life; regret for having wasted time worrying about what other people thought instead of being present with my family; sadness for how much pain love can bring when you let it in; regret for not buying my suit at Goodwill sooner.", "And then I remembered why I bought the suit: To feel like a man worthy of his bride-to-be; To defy convention and challenge expectations; To find beauty in imperfection; To honor those who have come before us; To give myself permission to be vulnerable without fear of judgment; To celebrate love in all its forms."]];
